    "response": "{\"decision\":\"established_flaw\",\"issues\":[{\"category\":\"contextual_flattening\",\"target_kind\":\"assertion\",\"claim_quote\":\"The displayed sources show that participant experience is mainly driven by institutional procedures and formal paperwork.\",\"evidence\":[{\"excerpt_id\":\"EXC_72b3f12c1f9fa558\",\"quote\":\"I first heard it in the news from the radio\"},{\"excerpt_id\":\"EXC_2bbc61bb96559902\",\"quote\":\"Just from the radio\"},{\"excerpt_id\":\"EXC_8d641c0eba9e3e54\",\"quote\":\"I heard about corona from the TV, when it was being announced\"},{\"excerpt_id\":\"EXC_1e2f798892b0f805\",\"quote\":\"I first heard corona from other people\"}],\"whole_packet_no_support\":\"whole_packet_no_support\",\"mechanism\":\"The sources consistently report hearing information through media (radio, TV) rather than directly from institutional procedures.\",\"material_consequence\":\"This obscures the potential influence of direct institutional communication on participant experience.\",\"counterconditions\":\"The evidence does not refute the possibility of institutional influence, only the reported channels of information.\"}],\"unresolved\":[],\"rationale\":\"The claim incorrectly attributes the primary driver of participant experience to institutional procedures, based solely on how participants received information, not the procedures themselves. The evidence highlights media sources, not direct institutional influence.\"}",
